        {
          "@id": "urn:uuid:c5707560-b9fb-505e-bce2-8148f4c08d26",
          "@type": "uco-observable:ExtractedStringsFacet",
          "uco-observable:strings": [
            {
              "@id": "urn:uuid:6551b5ef-d310-5bdf-8f4e-4a49aef0a4ee",
              "@type": "uco-observable:ExtractedString",
              "uco-observable:stringValue": "Case 3:23-cr-00031-DJH Document 23 Filed 10/09/23 Page 1 of 8 PageID #: 92\n\nUNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT\nWESTERN DISTRICT OF KENTUCKY\nAT LOUISVILLE\n\nUNITED STATES OF AMERICA PLAINTIFF\n\nvs. CRIMINAL ACTION NO. 3:23CR31-DJH\n\nSTEVEN B. EARNEST DEFENDANT\n\n-Electronically Filed-\nSENTENCING MEMORANDUM\n\nThe United States of America, by counsel, files this memorandum in support of\n\nsentencing which is currently scheduled for October 9, 2023. Due to the previously-disclosed\n\nobjection to the Presentence Investigation Report (PSR), we anticipate calling one witness,\n\nDetective Matt Hedden. It is anticipated that his direct testimony will take no longer than 15-20\n\nminutes. Consistent with the Plea Agreement, the United States will ask the Court to impose a\n\nsentence at the lowest end of the applicable United States Sentencing Guideline range, but not\n\nless than the mandatory minimum term of imprisonment required by law. The Plea Agreement is\n\nsilent regarding the appropriate term of Supervised Release. For the reasons set forth more fully\n\nbelow, the United States will request a life term of Supervised Release with special conditions\n\napplicable to individuals convicted of child sexual exploitation crimes. Finally, the United States\n\nwill request entry of an Order of Forfeiture for the digital device seized from Earnest at the time\n\nof his arrest (and used during commission of the crimes of conviction), as well as the $500.00\n\ncash he brought to the agreed upon site as down-payment for the child sex trafficking.\n\nGUIDELINES CALCULATION\n\nThe United States objects to the final PSR. (DN 21), namely its failure to include a 5-\n\nlevel enhancement pursuant to U.S.S.G. \u00a7 4B1.5(b). Based on the facts and circumstances\n\nsurrounding Earnest\u2019s current crimes of conviction as well as his admission to prior prohibited\nCase 3:23-cr-00031-DJH Document 23 Filed 10/09/23 Page 2 of 8 PageID #: 93\n\nsexual conduct with a minor, U.S.S.G. \u00a7 4B1.5 \u2013 Repeat and Dangerous Sex Offender Against\n\nMinors applies to the instant case. See U.S.S.G. \u00a7 4B1.5 Commentary, Application Notes 1,\n\n2, and 4 (including reference to 18 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 2426(b)(1)(A) & (B)). Consequently, the offense\n\nlevel should be increased by 5 levels. See U.S.S.G. \u00a7 4B1.5(b)(1).\n\nEarnest\u2019s crimes of conviction (18 U.S.C. \u00a7 1591(a)(1) \u2013 attempted sex trafficking of\n\nchildren; and 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2422(b) \u2013 attempted online enticement), are \u201ccovered sex crimes,\u201d for\n\npurposes of U.S.S.G. \u00a7 4B1.5(b). See U.S.S.G. \u00a7 4B1.5 Commentary, Application Note 2.\n\nNeither U.S.S.G. \u00a7 4B1.1 nor subsection (a) of U.S.S.G. \u00a7 4B1.5. However, Earnest engaged in a\n\npatter or activity involving prohibited sexual conduct.\n\nWith regard to application of Subsection (b), the definition of \u201cprohibited sexual\n\nconduct\u201d includes any offense described in 18 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 2426(b)(1)(A) or (B). See U.S.S.G. \u00a7\n\n4B1.5 Commentary, Application Note 4(A). In pertinent part, the covered offenses include \u201c(A)\n\nunder this chapter [117], chapter 109A, chapter 110, or section 1591; or (B) under State law for\n\nan offense consisting of conduct that would have been an offense under a chapter referred to in\n\nsubparagraph (A) if the conduct had occurred within the special maritime and territorial\n\njurisdiction of the United States.\u201d 18 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 2426(b)(1).\n\nA pattern of activity involves \u201cprohibited sexual conduct if on at least two separate\n\noccasions, the defendant engaged in prohibited sexual conduct with a minor.\u201d See U.S.S.G. \u00a7\n\n4B1.5 Commentary, Application Note 4(B)(i). Moreover, an occasion of prohibited sexual\n\nconduct may be considered for purposes of subsection (b) regardless of when it occurred or\n\nwhether it resulted in a conviction for the conduct that occurred on that occasion. See U.S.S.G. \u00a7\n\n4B1.5 Commentary, Application Note 4(B)(ii).\n\nDuring his post-Miranda interview, and again in the post-polygraph interview, Earnest\n\nadmits a longstanding sexual interest in children. He admits a significant interest in and\n\n2\nCase 3:23-cr-00031-DJH Document 23 Filed 10/09/23 Page 3 of 8 PageID #: 94\n\nacquisition of sexually explicit images of children that dates back many years. However, of\n\nsignificance to application of \u00a7 4B1.5 are the admissions regarding his conduct as a 16 (nearly\n\n17)-year-old who \u2013 along with a friend \u2013 engaged in sexual contact with the friend\u2019s nine-year-\n\nold sister. See 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2244(c); Ky. Rev. Stat. 510.110(1)(b) (sexual abuse in first degree \u2013\n\nperson subjects another person to sexual contact who is incapable of consent because he or she is\n\nless than 12 years old). Specifically, Earnest touched the bare breast of the nine-year-old girl as\n\nhis friend raised her nightgown and exposed her breasts. See 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2246(3)(definition of\n\nsexual contact).\n\nClearly, Earnest engaged in prohibited sexual conduct on at least two occasions. All of\n\nthe requirements for the five-level enhancement are met in the case at bar. However, the PSR\n\naddendum suggests that the 4B1.5 enhancement doesn\u2019t apply because one of the occasions\n\noccurred when Earnest was a juvenile. Contrary to the assertion in the addendum (DN 21-1), the\n\nSixth Circuit Court of Appeals held otherwise in United States v. Hassell, 763 Fed.Appx. 534 (6th\n\nCir. 2019). Hassell involved denial of a 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 2255 wherein the movant/defendant\n\nclaimed, in part, that the District Court had improperly calculated his guidelines by applying the\n\n4B1.5 enhancement based on the defendant\u2019s juvenile conduct. Id. at 535. The Sixth Circuit\n\ndispensed with that argument due to the appellate waiver in the plea agreement. Id. However,\n\nHassell also claimed that his counsel had been ineffective for failing to object to the\n\nenhancement on the same grounds as the assignment of error to the District Court. Id. Writing\n\nfor the panel, Judge Rogers noted that \u201cit is far from clear that the district court erred in the first\n\nplace in applying an enhancement on the basis of juvenile conduct in this case.\u201d Id. at 536. The\n\ndefendant had pointed to no controlling authority to support the position that juvenile conduct\n\ndoes not count for establishing a \u201cpattern\u201d of conduct under 4B1.5. Id. In the cases cited by\n\nHassell, other courts had determined that juvenile adjudications could not be considered as\n\n\u201cconvictions\u201d under 4B1.5(a). Id. However, the Second Circuit concluded that a district court\n3\nCase 3:23-cr-00031-DJH Document 23 Filed 10/09/23 Page 4 of 8 PageID #: 95\n\nwas permitted to take into account sexually exploitative conduct that occurred while the\n\ndefendant was himself juvenile when applying 4B1.5(b). Id. (citing United States v. Phillips,\n\n431 F.3d 86 (2d Cir. 2005). Ultimately, the Sixth Circuit determined that the cases cited by\n\nHassell did not show clear error by the district court to which counsel should have objected, \u201cif\n\nthey add up to any error at all.\u201d Hassell, 763 Fed.Appx. at 536.\n\nA closer examination of Phillips provides further insight into the Second Circuit\u2019s\n\nanalysis. The District Court applied the five-level enhancement under U.S.S.G. \u00a7 4B1.5(b) based\n\non unadjudicated juvenile conduct. Phillips, 431 F.3d at 87. \u201cUnadjudicated juvenile conduct\n\nmay be properly considered under \u00a7 4B1.5(b) [but the factual findings were insufficient].\u201d Id. at\n\n89. Recognizing that \u00a7 4B1.5(a) relies on the existence of at least one prior conviction for a sex\n\noffense, the Second Circuit went on to note that \u00a7 4B1.5(b) does not. Moreover, the list of\n\noffenses set out in 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2426 includes offenses that plainly extend to juvenile offenders.\n\nId. at 91. Of note, 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2423 includes criminal conduct committed by a 16-year-old, if\n\nthe minor is 12. Id.; see also, 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2423.\n\nThe Phillips court went on to observe that the pattern of activity involving prohibited\n\nsexual conduct also includes an offense under state law if it would have been an offense under\n\nthe applicable federal statutes. Id. The court noted that under New York law (where Phillips\u2019\n\njuvenile conduct occurred), also allowed for prosecution of juveniles. Id.\n\nFinally, the Phillips court turned directly to the language of the Sentencing Guidelines.\n\n\u201cSection 4B1.5(b) and its Application Notes contain no juvenile-conduct or conviction-limiting\n\nlanguage, in contrast to other Guidelines within Chapter 4.\u201d Id. at 92. By way of example, the\n\nCourt looked at \u00a7 4A1.1 and the Guidelines\u2019 directives for calculating criminal history,\n\nspecifically pertaining to juvenile adjudications. Id. Of note, \u201c[s]ection 4B1.5(b) makes no such\n\ndistinction between adult and juvenile adjudications [conduct].\u201d Id. at 92.\n\n4\nCase 3:23-cr-00031-DJH Document 23 Filed 10/09/23 Page 5 of 8 PageID #: 96\n\nBecause some of the offenses covered by 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2426(b)(1) prohibit\njuveniles from engaging in sexual acts with minors and because U.S.S.G. \u00a7 4B1.5(b) does\nnot specifically carve out unadjudicated juvenile conduct from the district court\u2019s\nconsideration, we conclude that, in applying \u00a7 4B1.5(b), the district court is permitted to\ntake into account sexually exploitative conduct that occurred while the defendant was\nhimself a juvenile.\n\nId. The Phillips\u2019 also decision makes sense because the Guideline section at issue is aimed at\n\nrepeat and dangerous sex offenders against minors.\n\nThe total offense level should be raised by five levels, pursuant to U.S.S.G. \u00a7 4B1.5(b),\n\nresulting in a total offense level of 38. The United States agrees with the criminal history\n\ncalculation in the PSR. With a total offense level of 38 and a criminal history category I, the\n\ndefendant\u2019s recommended Sentencing Guideline range is 235-293 months\u2019 imprisonment.\n\nSENTENCING FACTORS\n\nThe Court must ultimately affix a sentence which is sufficient, but not greater than\n\nnecessary, to comply with the purposes set forth in 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3553(a). The factors for the\n\nCourt to consider when imposing a sentence include:\n\n(1) the nature and circumstances of the offense and the history and characteristics of\nthe defendant;\n(2) the need for the sentence imposed:\n(A) to reflect the seriousness of the offense, to promote respect for the law, and to\nprovide just punishment for the offense;\n(B) to afford adequate deterrence to criminal conduct;\n(C) to protect the public from further crimes of the defendant; and\n(D) to provide the defendant with needed educational or vocational training, medical\ncare, or other correctional treatment in the most effective manner;\n(3) the kinds of sentences available;\n(4) the kinds of sentence and the sentencing range established for--\n(A) the applicable category of offense committed by the applicable category of\ndefendant as set forth in the guidelines--\n...\n(5) any pertinent policy statement--\n\n5\nCase 3:23-cr-00031-DJH Document 23 Filed 10/09/23 Page 6 of 8 PageID #: 97\n\n...\n(6) the need to avoid unwarranted sentence disparities among defendants with similar\nrecords who have been found guilty of similar conduct; and\n(7) the need to provide restitution to any victims of the offense.\n\nWhen applying those factors to this case a 180 \u2013 235 month sentence is sufficient, but not greater\n\nthan necessary, to meet the objectives of 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3553(a). Any further variances or\n\ndepartures are inappropriate.\n\nEarnest is a 35-year-old man who, by his personal accounts, enduring an extremely\n\ndifficult childhood. He obtained a G.E.D., served in the United States Armed Forces for four and\n\none-half years after which he received an honorable discharge. He obtained a CDL in 2012 and\n\nfor the past two years preceding his arrest, he owned his own truck and worked as a self-\n\nemployed contractor.\n\nHe communicated online with a sex worker during which he asked to pay for sex with a child.\n\nHe took steps in an effort to confirm that the child existed and agreed upon a price. After less\n\nthan two days of communications, Earnest drove to the agreed upon meeting place with $500.00\n\ncash in order to pay for sex with the child. During his post-Miranda / post-polygraph interviews,\n\nhe made several admissions about his sexual interest in children, acquisition of child sex\n\nexploitation materials, and prior hands-on offending with a nine-year-old girl when he was 16\n\n(almost 17).\n\nThe harmful effects of child sexual exploitation are well-documented by medical and\n\nmental health professionals. Congressional records related to changes in federal child sexual\n\nexploitation laws chronicle these harms as well as Congress\u2019 efforts to protect children and\n\npunish those who harm them. Earnest\u2019s sustained sexual interest in children is troubling and\n\nindicative of his dangerousness.\n\nA sentence of at least 180 \u2013 235 months reflects the seriousness of the offenses, promotes\n\nrespect for the law and provides just punishment. The lengthy prison sentence, when combined\n6\nCase 3:23-cr-00031-DJH Document 23 Filed 10/09/23 Page 7 of 8 PageID #: 98\n\nwith a life term of Supervised Release will serve as a deterrent to Earnest and others who may\n\ncontemplate similar conduct. Of significant importance, it will also protect the public from\n\nfurther crimes of the defendant. The recommended sentence does not run afoul of the need to\n\navoid sentencing disparities, in light of individualized sentencing.\n\nAs noted in the Plea Agreement and PSR, restitution is mandatory in this case, under 18\n\nU.S.C. \u00a7 2259. However, due to the undercover operation, there is no specifically identifiable\n\nvictim. Consequently, restitution is not appropriate.\n\nWe do not seek imposition of a fine or costs of incarceration. The mandatory $5,000.00\n\n(per count), special penalty assessment under 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3014 is applicable in the case at bar.\n\nWe defer to the Court\u2019s determination regarding Earnest\u2019s ability to pay. A motion for\n\npreliminary Order of Forfeiture will be filed.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nFor the reasons set forth herein, the United States respectfully requests that the Court\n\nimpose a sentence of between 180 - 235 months\u2019 imprisonment followed by a life term of\n\nSupervised Release.\n\nRespectfully submitted,\n\nMICHAEL A. BENNETT\nUnited States Attorney\n\n/s/ Jo E. Lawless\nJo E. Lawless\nAssistant United States Attorney\n717 West Broadway\nLouisville, Kentucky 40202\n(502) 625-7065\n(502) 582-5097 (Fax)\n\n7\nCase 3:23-cr-00031-DJH Document 23 Filed 10/09/23 Page 8 of 8 PageID #: 99\n\nCertificate of Service\n\nI hereby certify that a copy of the foregoing was sent by electronic transmission through\nthe Court\u2019s ECF system to defense counsel on October 9, 2023.\n\n/s/ Jo E. Lawless\nJo E. Lawless\nAssistant United States Attorney\n\n8"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
